.TH NZLAUNCHER 1 2026-11-14 nzlauncher "User Commands"
.SH NAME
nzlauncher \- a launcher that reads sentences and learns how you use it
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B nzlauncher
.I command
.RI [ arguments ]
.br
.B nzlauncher
.BR \-h " | " \-\-help " | " \-V " | " \-\-version
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B nzlauncher
opens applications, windows and files, and changes system settings, from a
single input. What is typed is read as a sentence, in any word order and
with typos forgiven:
.IR "wifi off" ,
.IR "volume 40" ,
.IR "pdfs from last week" ,
.IR "remind me in 20 minutes to stretch" ,
.IR "don't sleep until 6pm" .
A near miss suggests what it meant; anything unmatched falls back to a
calculation, a command to run or a web search.
.PP
Results are ranked by what you pick, when and where: the hour, the Wi\-Fi
network and the window you came from. A daemon
.RB ( "nzlauncher serve" )
keeps the engine warm and holds what has to outlive one command, such as
keep awake. The faces (terminal and GTK) and the scripting commands all talk
to it, and work without it at a cost in speed.
.SH COMMANDS
.SS Faces
.TP
.BR bar " [\fIquery\fR]"
Open the launcher with
.I query
typed in, or close it if it is open, in the face the
.B interface
setting of config.toml picks. Bind this to a key.
.TP
.BR tui " [\fIquery\fR]"
Open the terminal face in the current terminal.
.TP
.BR gtk " [\fIquery\fR]"
Open the GTK face. Needs
.B nzlauncher\-gtk
next to this binary or on
.BR PATH .
.SS Pickers
.TP
.BR pick " [\fB\-p\fR \fIprompt\fR] [\fB\-i\fR]"
Read lines from standard input, let the user choose one in a floating
picker, and print it.
.B \-i
also accepts typed text that matches no line. A closed picker prints
nothing.
.TP
.BR dmenu " [\fB\-p\fR \fIprompt\fR] [\fIdmenu flags\fR]"
A dmenu replacement: prints the choice, or the typed text. Other dmenu flags
are accepted and ignored. Linked or copied as
.BR dmenu ,
the binary is this command.
.SS Engine
.TP
.BR query " [\fItext\fR]"
Print the results for
.I text
one per line: id, command and display, separated by tabs.
.TP
.B serve
Run the daemon. The systemd user unit
.B nzlauncher.service
does this.
.TP
.B setup
Show what was found on this machine and what drives each capability.
.SS System
.TP
.BI "wifi connect " ssid
Connect to a known or open network.
.TP
.BI "wifi join " ssid
Join a new secured network, asking for the passphrase in a dialog (iwd, with
zenity or kdialog). nzlauncher answers iwd as its agent, so the passphrase
never reaches a command line.
.TP
.B wifi disconnect
Leave the current network.
.TP
.B wifi list
Print the networks in range, one name per line.
.TP
.BI "tweak power " profile
Switch the power profile:
.BR power\-saver ,
.BR balanced ,
.BR performance .
Needs power\-profiles\-daemon, tlp\-pd or tuned\-ppd.
.TP
.BR "tweak dnd on" | off
Do not disturb, on mako, swaync or dunst.
.TP
.BR "tweak nightlight " \fIkelvin\fR | off
Warm the screen, 1000 to 10000 kelvin, with hyprsunset.
.TP
.BR "tweak awake " [ screen | sleep | all "] " on | off | \fIseconds\fR
Keep awake.
.B screen
stops idle locking and blanking.
.B sleep
keeps the machine from suspending, lid closed included, while the screen may
still lock.
.B all
is both. A number of seconds holds it for that long,
.B on
until turned off;
.B off
lets go. Without a hold name it is
.BR screen .
The daemon holds these.
.TP
.B tweak normal
Let go of every keep awake hold and switch to the balanced power profile.
.TP
.BR "catalog status" | encrypt | decrypt | edit
Keep the catalog in plain text, the default, or encrypted so that only
nzlauncher opens it.
.B encrypt
turns catalog.toml into catalog.toml.enc and removes the plain file;
.B decrypt
goes back;
.B edit
opens it in
.B $VISUAL
or
.BR $EDITOR ,
decrypting an encrypted one to
.B $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR
(memory) and encrypting it again on save. The key comes from the
.B key
setting in the
.B [catalog]
table of config.toml:
.B file
(the default: a key nzlauncher makes and keeps apart from the config),
.BI file: path\fR,
.B keyring
(the Secret Service),
.B op://
followed by a 1Password reference, or
.BR passphrase .
A key file or the keyring protects the catalog wherever it is copied or
synced, but not from programs running as you, which can read the key too;
1Password and a passphrase need your approval or your memory.
.TP
.BI "focus " window
Focus the first window whose class or title matches.
.TP
.BR ws " [\fBmove\fR] \fIn\fR"
Go to workspace
.IR n ,
or with
.BR move ,
send the focused window there.
.SS Help
.TP
.BR help " [\fIcommand\fR]"
Print the overview, or a command's arguments. Every command also takes
.B \-h
and
.BR \-\-help .
.TP
.B man
Print this page as roff.
.TP
.BR completion " " zsh | bash | fish
Print a completion script.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print usage and exit.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Print the version and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-
End the options: what follows is an argument even if it starts with a
dash, as in
.BR "nzlauncher query \-\- \-\-help" .
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success, or a picker closed without a choice.
.TP
.B 1
A failure: the service did not answer, the network refused, and the like.
.TP
.B 2
A usage error.
.TP
.B 3
.B wifi join
cannot ask for a passphrase here; use the Wi\-Fi service's own tool.
.TP
.B 4
.B wifi join
was canceled in the dialog.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B NZ_FOCUSED
The class of the window the user came from, which feeds the ranking. The
bar sets it for its faces.
.TP
.B NZ_NO_IMAGES
When set, the terminal face draws no image previews.
.TP
.BR XDG_CONFIG_HOME ", " XDG_DATA_HOME ", " XDG_CACHE_HOME ", " XDG_RUNTIME_DIR
Where the files below live.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/nzlauncher/config.toml
Settings. Moving it away once makes nzlauncher write a commented template.
.TP
.I ~/.config/nzlauncher/catalog.toml
Your own actions, read fresh on every launch.
.TP
.I ~/.config/nzlauncher/catalog.toml.enc
The catalog, encrypted (AES\-256\-GCM). When it is there, it is the catalog.
.TP
.I ~/.local/share/nzlauncher/catalog.key
The key for an encrypted catalog with the default key setting. Keep a copy:
without it the catalog cannot be opened.
.TP
.I ~/.config/nzlauncher/providers.d/
Executable plugins: the first word of a query names one.
.TP
.I ~/.local/share/nzlauncher/
What has been learned: events.jsonl and frecency.json.
.TP
.I ~/.cache/nzlauncher/
What can be made again: decoded images, rates.
.TP
.I $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/nzlauncher.sock
The daemon's socket.
.SH EXAMPLES
Open the launcher on a key, in hyprland.lua:
.PP
.RS
.nf
hl.bind("SUPER + SPACE", hl.dsp.exec_cmd("nzlauncher bar"))
.fi
.RE
.PP
Choose a wallpaper in a script:
.PP
.RS
.nf
ls ~/Pictures | nzlauncher pick \-p Wallpaper
.fi
.RE
.PP
Keep the machine running for two hours with the screen allowed to lock:
.PP
.RS
.nf
nzlauncher tweak awake sleep 7200
.fi
.RE
.PP
Run the top result for a sentence:
.PP
.RS
.nf
nzlauncher query volume 40 | head \-1 | cut \-f2 | sh
.fi
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR systemctl (1),
.BR iwctl (1),
.BR hyprsunset (1),
.BR fzf (1)
.PP
Documentation: https://github.com/tuklu/nZlauncher/wiki
